The agent (Phase 4B PR B): a Nautobot Job, **Investigate an Event Ticket**, that reads one ticket, calls the read-only MCP tools an operator has enabled, and writes what it found back onto the ticket through the same service layer a human uses (rules A1-A10). `services/agent.py` holds the loop; `AgentRun` and `AgentToolCall` record what happened, and both are readable in the UI, the REST API and GraphQL. Plus an **Ollama** LLM provider type, reaching Ollama through its own API rather than its OpenAI-compatibility layer: that layer does not return tool calls in the `tool_calls` field, so an Ollama-backed agent on the OpenAI-compatible path could not call a tool at all. An MCP server's **description** now reaches the model, in the definition of every tool on that server, alongside the device platform of any object attached to the ticket. Both are places to write down what a model cannot be assumed to know: told only that a device exists, a local model asked a Nokia SR Linux box for `show ip interface brief` until it ran out of iterations, and told which platform it is and what its commands look like, it ran the right ones first time. Neither is a control - the allowlist is the control (rule M4).
